{
  "packet_id": "79cf11084400ffea",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 lists alternatives (no expansion, a different route to buyers, a new customer group), which partly separates the objective from the suggested solution. But the objective largely restates the question, and the alternatives are generic. Alternative 1 offers 'industrial' as a consumer use case, which is confused. A1 also asserts existing 'face-protection offerings', which no source supports: hlb-head speaks of head protection. That unsupported company claim caps the dimension at 1. The framing also misses hlb-junior, which shows Hellberg already markets a consumer, use-case-specific product (children, concerts, lawn mowing). That would have corrected the question's premise."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "A9 states who changes behaviour (consumers who come to see hearing protection as a lifestyle accessory) and why (they buy multiple pairs for different activities). It names value capture through premium pricing and lifestyle channels. The chain stays generic and speculative. It never shows why this manufacturer specifically would capture the value, given hlb-owner's roughly 20 employees and SEK 66m in sales."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T2 (A10) is essentially a rewording of T1 (A9). Both describe fashion and lifestyle demand, use the same value capture, use the same channels and make identical predictions. Neither theory implies a different product, customer or channel."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Assumptions are stated but vague. The predictions are partly observable (survey intent), but 'sales data… in lifestyle channels' presumes channels that do not exist. 'Price elasticity will be positive, indicating consumers are sensitive to price' is internally incoherent. No prediction would put T1 in trouble specifically rather than T2."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14"],
      "reason": "A11, A12 and A14 label their sources as company assertions and say what each excerpt does not show, which is a partial strength. But only hlb-head and uvx-consumer are assessed. A14 does not note that uvx-consumer appears to describe a different company's capabilities rather than Hellberg's. The directly relevant hlb-junior (an existing consumer use-case product), hlb-tech (Bluetooth and FM-radio models) and hlb-retail are never assessed. No assessment addresses date, place or fit with the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "A13's 'challenge' query is a generic topical search, not a targeted search for evidence against T1. Only one hit (uvx-consumer) is assessed, in A14, as neutral. The run never explicitly reports that it found no disconfirming evidence. T2 is never challenged."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A15"],
      "reason": "A15 is billed as a test between T1 and T2, but it states identical predictions for both, so it cannot separate them. The decision rule is incoherent: 'If both conditions fail → no test'. The thresholds are unexplained assumptions. The test measures stated intent, not willingness to pay. Cost and timeline fit the budget, but the test does not discriminate between the theories."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A17"],
      "reason": "A17 correctly identifies SYN-D as simulated and irrelevant (a warehouse label printer, with no wearers or buyers measured). It keeps T1 without adding a claim, which is a proportionate, evidence-linked retention. It does not explicitly trace any implication for the recommendation, but none was needed."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A16", "A18"],
      "reason": "A18 takes a position (do not invest), but it is internally inconsistent:\n- It 'favours T1' while rating T1 'unlikely'. That belief is not supported, because every assessment of T1 (A11, A14) was neutral.\n- The next action (run the €8,000 test) is truncated and cites a nonexistent 'X1'.\n- The reversal conditions (≤50% / ≤60% → 'reconsider the case for a differentiated proposition') contradict A15's decision rule. They are backwards: test failure would support the current no-investment stance, not reverse it.\n- It asserts 'face-protection' offerings without support."
    }
